 ],
 "literal": "If there is gain, befriending; if it ceases, abandoning; the friendship of those who have no comparison, even if gained, even if lost, what is the use?",
 "translation": "What does it matter whether we gain or lose the friendship of those incomparable people who befriend us only when there is gain and abandon us when it ceases?",
 "modern": "பயன் கிடைக்கும்போது நட்புக்கொண்டு, பயன் இல்லாதபோது விலகிப்போகும் ஒப்பில்லாத சுயநலவாதிகளின் நட்பைப் பெறுவதனால் என்ன பயன்? அதனை இழப்பதனால் என்ன நஷ்டம்?",
 "explanation": "This couplet describes the nature of fair-weather friends who seek association solely for their own benefit. The word 'uṟiṉ' means when there is profit or gain, and 'aṟiṉ' means when that profit ceases. The word 'ṅaru'um' (a variant of 'orū'um') means to abandon or distance oneself. Such people are described as 'oppilār'—incomparable in their extreme selfishness. The poet asks what difference it makes ('eṉ') whether we gain ('peṟiṉum') or lose ('iỻappiṉum') their friendship, as it is completely worthless in either case.",
